{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And its drink-offering</b> — wine. <b>In the holy place pour it out</b> — on the altar they shall pour it. <b>A drink-offering of strong drink</b> — intoxicating wine, excluding wine straight from the press — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 28:7). But this is not correct, for the Sages have already stated (Bava Batra 97a): one may not bring wine straight from the press, but if he did bring it, it is valid. Now this is not excluded by the verse at all; rather, it is a rabbinic stringency for the initial obligation (le-chatchilah). But <b>strong drink</b> — this serves to exclude diluted wine, which is disqualified even after the fact (be-di'avad). And so it is expounded in the Sifrei (Pinchas 23:33), where they said: \"In the holy place pour it out\" — that they are poured out in the holy place and in the holy place they are absorbed.\n\nThe verse mentioned in the daily offering (tamid) and in the new moons the measure of fine flour in the meal-offering and the measure of wine in the libations. But in the section of the Sabbath and the Festival of Unleavened Bread and Shavuot and Rosh HaShanah and Yom HaKippurim and the first day of the Festival of Sukkot, it mentioned the measure of the meal-offering but did not mention the measure of the libations.\n\nThe reason for this is that although He commanded in the section of the libations (above, chapter 15) the measures of the meal-offering and the libations for a bull, for a ram, and for the lambs, whether for vows and freewill offerings or for the appointed times (ibid. v. 3), we find a doubled meal-offering for the lamb of the Omer (Leviticus 23:13), and on the day of Atzeret two tenths, leavened (ibid. v. 17), and likewise in the thanksgiving offering (ibid. 7:12–13), and these are meal-offerings. Therefore the verse needed to specify for all the appointed times that the meal-offering is not changed in them as it is changed in those. But the libations never change; therefore He mentioned them at the outset in the tamid and did not mention them afterward in the Sabbath, and then He again mentioned them on the new moon, to include also the libations of the bull and the rams, and after that there was no need to mention them at all. And He mentioned on the first day of Sukkot the measure of the meal-offering (Numbers 29:14), and there was no further need to mention in that festival anything other than \"their meal-offerings and their libations according to their number, as their ordinance\" — that is, as their ordinance on the first day that is mentioned."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "Ramban cites the Sifrei by the sedra name 'Pinchas'; the reference 23:33 follows the common numbering of Sifrei Zuta on Numbers, chapter 23."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:09:34.737530379Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}